WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:12.600
It is two in the morning. Bima is finishing a report that is due at nine. Again.

2
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:21.100
He has tried the fixes: a planner, an app, an earlier alarm. Each one lasted about a week.

3
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:27.600
The fixes fail because they are practices. The problem sits one layer down.

4
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.600
Think of three layers.

5
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:35.500
Practices are what you do: the planner, the alarm.

6
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:42.800
Paradigms are the lens you see work through. His says: only pressure makes me work.

7
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:51.700
Principles stay true whether he believes them or not. Skill follows steady practice, and tired work is worse work.

8
00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:59.800
So he audits the lens. What would I have to believe for all-night work to make sense?

9
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:08.700
Then he tests that belief against evidence. His two best pieces of work this year were both started early.

10
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:16.800
A new lens. A start I schedule beats a deadline I fear. The practices follow by themselves.

11
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.300
One more idea before the next lessons. Growth at work follows an order.

12
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.600
You start directed. Teachers, parents and luck decide the outcome.

13
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:34.300
Then self-directed. You own your responses, your direction and your schedule.

14
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:40.800
Only then are you trusted with others. The middle step cannot be skipped.

15
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:49.700
Three weeks later. It is Monday afternoon, the report is due on Thursday, and it is already half done.

16
00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:57.800
He did not find a better app. He changed the lens, and many practices changed at once.

17
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.300
Practices copied without their lens fade in weeks.

18
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:06.800
A changed lens changes many practices at once.

19
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:15.800
Now it is your turn. Take your least effective habit and ask: what would I have to believe for this to make sense?
